`check schema` is the offline half of the decode seam: one payload against
one type of a zk2 revision (#612, FJ8b), which "needs no session at all —
that is the whole point". It is also the clearest place in the tool where the
0/1/2 contract carries three *different* facts rather than degrees of one,
which is why it sits under `check` with the others (#307).

The type is the revision's own — from `--contracts` here, so the session is
never opened (the `--zenoh-config` points at a dead bus and nothing fails),
or retrieved from its holders (spec §8.4). A JSON Schema type decodes as JSON
or CBOR and is validated (§7.3); a protobuf type decodes through the
bundle's descriptor set (§7.2).

**0 — it conforms.**

```console
$ zenctl check schema tc.netif.v1 state/interfaces/{ns}/{iface} --contracts ../examples/zk2/.history --zenoh-config tests/cmd/fixtures/dead-bus.json5 --from '{"name":"eth0","index":2,"namespace":"default","is_up":true}'
{"conformance":{"state":"valid"},"declared":"json:NetworkInterface","fingerprint":"sha256:4f531ebec4c4eee015c18f11aefbce9138b413cfdca6bf6b777ea0eb6f43b4e0","iface":"tc.netif.v1","member":"type","report":"schema-check","resource":"state/interfaces/{ns}/{iface}","size":60,"value":{"index":2,"is_up":true,"name":"eth0","namespace":"default"}}

```

**1 — it was checked, and it does not conform.** A finding, with the failing
constraint named by its JSON pointer.

```console
$ zenctl check schema tc.netif.v1 state/interfaces/{ns}/{iface} --contracts ../examples/zk2/.history --zenoh-config tests/cmd/fixtures/dead-bus.json5 --from '{"name":"eth0","index":2,"namespace":"default","is_up":"yes"}'
? 1
{"conformance":{"state":"invalid","violations":["/is_up: expected boolean, found string"]},"declared":"json:NetworkInterface","fingerprint":"sha256:4f531ebec4c4eee015c18f11aefbce9138b413cfdca6bf6b777ea0eb6f43b4e0","iface":"tc.netif.v1","member":"type","report":"schema-check","resource":"state/interfaces/{ns}/{iface}","size":61,"value":{"index":2,"is_up":"yes","name":"eth0","namespace":"default"}}

```

Bytes that do not decode as the declared type at all are the same finding,
not a different exit: what was declared did not arrive.

```console
$ zenctl check schema tc.netif.v1 state/interfaces/{ns}/{iface} --contracts ../examples/zk2/.history --zenoh-config tests/cmd/fixtures/dead-bus.json5 --from 'not json'
? 1
{"conformance":{"declared":"json:NetworkInterface","reason":"as json: expected ident at line 1 column 2","state":"undecodable"},"declared":"json:NetworkInterface","fingerprint":"sha256:4f531ebec4c4eee015c18f11aefbce9138b413cfdca6bf6b777ea0eb6f43b4e0","iface":"tc.netif.v1","member":"type","report":"schema-check","resource":"state/interfaces/{ns}/{iface}","size":8}

```

**2 — the check never happened.** Reserved so CI can tell "nonconformant" from
"unobservable" — the same split `check cutover` and `check probe` guard, and
the reason silence is never a verdict. A resource the revision does not
declare is the input refused:

```console
$ zenctl check schema tc.netif.v1 state/nope --contracts ../examples/zk2/.history --zenoh-config tests/cmd/fixtures/dead-bus.json5 --from '{}'
? 2
check schema: not checked: tc.netif.v1 declares no stream or state or event or operation resource "state/nope"; it declares: @op/diagnostics, @op/interfaces/{ns}/{iface}/set, state/interfaces/{ns}/{iface}, state/namespaces, stream/bandwidth/{ns}/{iface}
check schema: the question could not be asked — exit 2, the reserved non-verdict (1 here would claim a verdict this run never reached)

```

A member the resource does not have is the same: a stream sample has no
`request`.

```console
$ zenctl check schema tc.netif.v1 stream/bandwidth/{ns}/{iface} --member request --contracts ../examples/zk2/.history --zenoh-config tests/cmd/fixtures/dead-bus.json5 --from '{}'
? 2
check schema: not checked: stream/bandwidth/{ns}/{iface} declares no request
check schema: the question could not be asked — exit 2, the reserved non-verdict (1 here would claim a verdict this run never reached)

```

The payload that could not be read is the same fact, and used not to be: a
missing `@file` came back through `?` as exit **1**, telling CI a typo'd path
was a schema violation (#244). It is now what it always was — a check that
never happened.

The message names the flag and the interpretation, because the failure a user
actually hits here is a literal `@` they did not mean as a path.

```console
$ zenctl check schema tc.netif.v1 state/interfaces/{ns}/{iface} --contracts ../examples/zk2/.history --zenoh-config tests/cmd/fixtures/dead-bus.json5 --from @tests/cmd/fixtures/no-such-payload.json
? 2
check schema: not checked: --from: cannot read tests/cmd/fixtures/no-such-payload.json — a leading @ means "read this path": No such file or directory (os error 2)
check schema: the question could not be asked — exit 2, the reserved non-verdict (1 here would claim a verdict this run never reached)

```
